  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a subduction-zone earthquake generates a tsunami, how the source is characterized, and how that information is used for warning and long-term coastal planning. It shows an offshore fault rupture causing seafloor displacement, which lifts the overlying ocean and produces a tsunami that travels toward the coast. Sensors on the seafloor, buoys, and land-based seismic instruments monitor both the earthquake and the resulting ocean response.\n\nA central schematic of the plate boundary highlights the geometry of the tsunami source: a shallow-dip earthquake ruptures beneath an accretionary wedge, with splay faults, sediments, and rough plate topography influencing deformation. The rupture patch is drawn with its approximate length and width, and the figure emphasizes that uplift of the shallow seafloor near the coastline is the key driver of tsunami generation.\n\nThe lower part contrasts two practical uses of these observations. In the short term, a monitoring network feeds broadcasts and early-warning messages that support evacuation. In the longer term, historical seismic and tsunami data are used for inundation mapping and hazard assessment, which guide coastal protection, relocation, and evacuation planning. Overall, the image links earthquake mechanics offshore to tsunami risk management onshore.",
